uchi. <year>2005</year>. Manipulation of membrane protein topology on the endoplasmic reticulum by a specific ligand in living cells. <source>J. Biochem. (Tokyo).</source> <volume>138</volume>:<fpage>631</fpage>&#x02013;637.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16272575</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Jackson, R.J., and T. Hunt. <year>1983</year>. Preparation and use of nuclease-treated rabbit reticulocyte lysates for the translation of eukaryotic messenger RNA. <source>Methods Enzymol.</source> <volume>96</volume>:<fpage>50</fpage>&#x02013;74.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6656641</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Keefe, A.D., D.S. Wilson, B. Seelig, and J.W. Szostak. <year>2001</year>. One-step purification of recombinant proteins using a nanomolar-affinity streptavidin-binding peptide, the SBP-tag. <source>Protein Expr. Purif.</source> <volume>23</volume>:<fpage>440</fpage>&#x02013;446.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11722181</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Kida, Y., M. Sakaguchi, M. Fukuda, K. Mikoshiba, and K. Mihara. <year>2000</year>. Membrane topogenesis of a type I signal-anchor protein, mouse synaptotagmin II, on the endoplasmic reticulum. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>150</volume>:<fpage>719</fpage>&#x02013;730.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10952998</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Kida, Y., K. Mihara, and M. Sakaguchi. <year>2005</year>. Translocation of a long amino-terminal domain through ER membrane by following signal-anchor sequence. <source>EMBO J.</source> <volume>24</volume>:<fpage>3202</fpage>&#x02013;3213.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16107879</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Kida, Y., F. Morimoto, K. Mihara, and M. Sakaguchi. <year>2006</year>. Function of positive charges following signal-anchor sequences during translocation of the N-terminal domain. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>281</volume>:<fpage>1152</fpage>&#x02013;1158.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16291756</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Li, W., S. Schulman, D. Boyd, K. Erlandson, J. Beckwith, and T.A. Rapoport. <year>2007</year>. The plug domain of the SecY protein stabilizes the closed state of the translocation channel and maintains a membrane seal. <source>Mol. Cell.</source> <volume>26</volume>:<fpage>511</fpage>&#x02013;521.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17531810</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Lu, Y., I.R. Turnbull, A. Bragin, K. Carveth, A.S. Verkman, and W.R. Skach. <year>2000</year>. Reorientation of aquaporin-1 topology during maturation in the endoplasmic reticulum. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>11</volume>:<fpage>2973</fpage>&#x02013;2985.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10982394</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Manting, E.H., C. van Der Does, H. Remigy, A. Engel, and A.J. Driessen. <year>2000</year>. SecYEG assembles into a tetramer to form the active protein translocation channel. <source>EMBO J.</source> <volume>19</volume>:<fpage>852</fpage>&#x02013;861.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10698927</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Menetret, J.F., R.S. Hegde, S.U. Heinrich, P. Chandramouli, S.J. Ludtke, T.A. Rapoport, and C.W. Akey. <year>2005</year>. Architecture of the ribosome-channel complex derived from native membranes. <source>J. Mol. Biol.</source> <volume>348</volume>:<fpage>445</fpage>&#x02013;457.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15811380</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Mitra, K., C. Schaffitzel, T. Shaikh, F. Tama, S. Jenni, C.L. Brooks, N. Ban, and J. Frank. <year>2005</year>. Structure of the <italic>E. coli</italic> protein-conducting channel bound to a translating ribosome. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>438</volume>:<fp